    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\calM $ be the correspondence associated to the adjunction $(F,G)$. In other words, $\calM $ is a simplicial category containing $\calC $ and $\calD $ as full (simplicial) subcategories, with 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ \bHom _{\calM }(C,D) = \bHom _{\calC }(C,G(D)) = \bHom _{\calD }(F(C),D) \]
</div>
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{\calM }(D,C) = \emptyset  \]
</div>
    <p>
       for every pair of objects $C \in \calC $, $D \in \calD $. Let $M = \sNerve (\calM )$. Then $M$ is a correspondence between $\sNerve (\calC )$ and $\sNerve (\calD )$. By Proposition <a href="/tag/7D37B6" data-tag="7D37B6">5.2.4.4</a>, it is an adjunction. It is easy to see that this adjunction is associated to both $f$ and $g$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
